  You'd be amazed what can be useful during the Ascension Run,
especially in the Endgame.  Google for the thread "Endgame Hints."  I've
done a lot of posts in similar threads.  

  E.g.  Read a scroll of magic mapping when you enter the Elemental
Plane of Air.  That will map the clouds so you can stay out of them.
You can get frozen by lightning in the clouds with no way to resist it.
Ring of Free Action won't help, neither will shock resistance.

  Wands of teleport, jumping boots, and a good light source seem to be
non-obvious items that can save your Ascension in the Endgame.

>I will charge up my ring of protection as best I can, and my wands of
>death.

  Don't bother charging the ring of protection,  free action and
conflict are what you want.  (If you have positively enchanted rings of
protection, increase damage, or increase accuracy, consider polyselfing
and eating them, if they can be eaten.  See the ring spoiler.)

>I will wear the ring of protection on one hand, and the regeneration or
>free action as need on the other.

  You want to wear a ring of free action on one hand, and a ring of
conflict on the other.  Monsters fighting each other are not bothering
you.  Add in being invisible and/or displaced and the Endgame is a much
less dangerous place to be.  (Yes, give up the robe for displacement if
necessary.  Just as you shouldn't be wasting precious time fighting,
your shouldn't be spending time casting spells.)

  Zap yourself with a wand of make invisible just before you wake Rodney
at the very latest.  (After killing Rodney, level teleport or zap wands
of digging down to get to the VS as quickly as possible.)

  Alternatively, if you have the spell of charm monster and/or several
scrolls of taming you can read while confused, and a magic whistle, you
could tame a mighty army on the Planes and follow them to victory.
(Note that is approach is not really compatible with wearing a ring of
conflict.)

>I want to enchant/fooproof the boots of levitation(I don't want to rely
>on my ring it can explode).
>Is there any adventage to using the fireproofed boots of water walking
>over levitation? Should I even bring them?

  If you have the spell of jumping, they are perfect for the Elemental
Plane/Plain of Fire.  The Plane of Fire is unique in that it has
unlimited line of sight, and you can #jump there.  The unlimited line of
sight makes it ideal for summoning storms.  Remember that one
Archon/Titan/Foolich(?)/etc. can suddenly become a dozen.

  Where do the fireproof water walking boots come into it?  IF you have
the spell of jumping, a good light source (lantern or lamp) you can
travel three squares horizontally/vertically, or two diagonally, instead
of just one.  (NB: You'll burn a lot of mana this way.  Jumping boots
are probably better even on the Plane of Fire.  Just be *careful* of
lava.)

  A player who is just "fast" can jump across a level *and back* before
a "very fast" player can cross a level *once*.  Few monsters are a
threat when they are receding rapidly in your rear-view mirror.
(Remember the first rule of bad driving:  "What is behind you does not
count.")  

  There's also a hidden advantage to jumping: it's guaranteed if you
meet the requirements:

  - Path is not blocked
  - You can see the landing square.  
    - Lava squares are self-lit. (Do NOT jump onto lava square with 
      jumping boots!)
    - Some of the monsters on the Plane of Fire illuminate the squares 
      around them. 
    - Can't be blinded.

  Being fast or very fast only give you a probability of 50% (?) extra
movement in a turn.  Even very fast only gives a two-thirds probability
of that extra movement: 10% chance that any two consecutive turns will
not get any extra movement.  #jumping guarantees at least double or
triple traveling distance per turn.  If you do get that extra movement
per turn, that doubles your #jumping traveling distance again.

  Zap a wand of teleport to clear a path for jumping if necessary on the
Elemental Plane of Earth, and on the Astral Plane.

  Jumping boots are probably better since the Amulet of Yendor doubles
spell casting mana consumption, on the average.

  Players that have tried the zap /oTele then jump approach say it's so
easy it feels like cheating.  Good, the RNG deserves it.

